<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akova</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Matagrifon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akova was a castle in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, which the French called Matagrifon. Around 1209 it became the seat of one of the twelve baronies of the crusader Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-of-rosieres">Walter of Rosières</a>. A disputed inheritance of the barony in the 1270s became a famous case of feudal law, and in 1320 the Byzantine commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-asen">Andronikos Asen</a> captured the castle.</p></div>
